EVERY BREAKPOINT HALFWORD AND ITS CONDITIONAL-EXECUTION EXCEPTION.

Experimental English Major C ARMv7-A Thumb machine contract.
This document completely CLASSIFIES the 256 individual
sixteen-bit instruction halfwords BE00..BEFF.
It does NOT PERMIT any of them as an executable
source operation in the present language profile.

PINNED INSTRUCTION-SET SOURCES.

Primary:
ARM DDI 0406C.d, ID040418, Chapter A6.2.5
16-bit miscellaneous instructions, BKPT branch of
the decode table, and A8.8.14 "BKPT" (page A8-344).
The processor's actual response is detailed by its
system/debug extensions and the relevant part C
chapters about debug events and configured debug mode.

Official primary manual:
https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f8daeb7f86e16515cdb8c4e

Secondary:
Arm Instruction Set Assembly Guide for Armv7 and earlier
architectures, document 100076_0200_00_en, section C2.18
"BKPT". It confirms the Thumb sixteen-bit immediate
range, unconditional operation and debug-state purpose.
This supplemental guide does not replace DDI 0406C.d.

The complete row-by-row source classification is:
  _/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-breakpoint-halfwords.tsv
at the repository root. The TSV is data about an
instruction set. Reading it does not invoke a breakpoint.

PART ONE. THE CLOSED ENCODING FAMILY.

A T32 sixteen-bit BKPT T1 consists of:
  bits 15 through 8: fixed binary 10111110
  bits 7 through 0: eight-bit unsigned immediate.
The fixed high byte is hexadecimal BE.
The immediate takes every integer from 0 through 255.
There are exactly 256 distinct combinations.
The resulting halfword is 0xBE00 plus the immediate.

All 256 combinations are classified as BKPT T1.
All are TWO bytes long. The immediate is not a
signed branch displacement, register number,
memory address or new instruction-width indicator.

The entire finite region can be divided into:
  BE00..BE7F: immediate 0 through 127;
  BE80..BEFF: immediate 128 through 255.
The second half of this numeric range does NOT
become a negative immediate simply because the
most significant bit of the literal is one.
In this instruction the literal is UNSIGNED.

There is no conditional-branch destination
calculated from the immediate. There is
no implicit subroutine call, return address
or data-memory load determined by these eight bits.

PART TWO. WHY A RECOGNIZED BREAKPOINT DOES NOT EQUAL A GRANTED OPERATION.

The architecture has defined BKPT, but the
current 72-operation English Major C grant
does not contain a long operation authorizing
a BKPT debug event.

A native disassembler should still identify
each BE00..BEFF halfword as BKPT. A translator
that encounters such bytes must say what
instruction they represent, and then stop its
checked semantic translation at an explicitly
UNGRANTED DEBUG-EVENT boundary.

The disallowed source-level operation is not
a made-up NOP, not an undefined instruction,
not a supervisor service call, and not an ordinary
two-byte branch that may or may not be taken.

A future version might grant a long English
operation such as:
  "Request the architecture-defined breakpoint
   debug event, carrying this unsigned diagnostic
   immediate, and transfer control according to
   the exact target's debug configuration."
But that sentence alone is NOT a new permitted
operation until the exception and debug
environment have been fully specified,
independently checked and versioned.

PART THREE. WHAT THE BREAKPOINT DOES IF IT IS EXECUTED.

BKPT requests the architecture-defined breakpoint
debug event. It may cause a halt into a debugger
or generate a debug exception depending on actual
processor debug mode, security/debug state and OS
configuration. It cannot be universally reduced
to "the current user process receives SIGTRAP".
Nor does the source machine necessarily run a
user-written handler immediately after BKPT.

The immediate is available in the encoding and
can carry information useful to debugging tools.
ARM describes it as ignored for the hardware's
core breakpoint operation; this does not mean
the eight literal bits are nonexistent.

The detailed effect on processor state, fault or
debug records, exception entry, saved status,
the instruction to which control may return, and
whether execution resumes at all depends on a
separate real-device/OS debug declaration.

The English Major C machine model has not yet
granted access to the debug configuration,
exception-control state or an OS handler.
It therefore may not claim a normal successful
execution-index successor merely by knowing
that the BKPT encoding occupies two bytes.

PART FOUR. BKPT DOES NOT OBEY ORDINARY IT PREDICATION.

This is the most important execution distinction.

A T32 BKPT is an ALWAYS-UNCONDITIONAL
instruction. If the CPU encounters it inside
an otherwise active T32 IT block, BKPT is
not suppressed by a false IT condition.
An actual executable BKPT can still request
its debug event, regardless of whether
the surrounding IT predicate evaluates
true or false.

This is DIFFERENT from an ordinary
register-copy instruction governed by ITSTATE,
whose write can be suppressed when the
condition is false.

This is also DIFFERENT from a T1 conditional B
which is architecturally forbidden inside
an IT block. "Can appear in IT" and
"has conditionally suppressed effects in IT"
are NOT logically equivalent statements.

Consequently, the following conceptual
attempt is not a valid way to conditionally
request a breakpoint:

  Establish IT EQ with one following slot.
  Encode BKPT as that following instruction.
  Assume BKPT is skipped when Z is zero.

The assumption is false in the architecture.
A checked source language must either reject
the attempted ungranted BKPT or model the
unconditional debug event explicitly.
It must not claim a false IT predicate
makes an ungranted instruction harmless.

PART FIVE. HOW TO AVOID A BREAKPOINT ON ONE PATH.

A distinct, valid assembly arrangement can put
a conditional BRANCH ahead of a BKPT and transfer
control past its entire two-byte encoding when
the chosen condition holds. In the path where
that branch is taken, the architectural execution
index never selects the BKPT instruction, so
the BKPT is not architecturally executed.

That arrangement uses an actual branch instruction
and can be described with the existing permitted
T1 conditional-branch operation 43, provided
the branch lies OUTSIDE IT, has a valid target
and remains inside its permitted range.

The two arrangements are not interchangeable:
  (a) execute a BKPT in a false IT slot;
  (b) take a branch around the BKPT entirely.

A hardware instruction fetch unit might
speculatively fetch bytes along an untaken
path. Fetching an instruction-looking halfword
does not itself mean that instruction's
architectural debug event occurred.

PART SIX. DISTINGUISH BKPT, UDF AND SVC.

The three native encodings represent
three distinct architectural mechanisms:

BKPT T1: BE00..BEFF, debug event.
UDF T1: DE00..DEFF, undefined instruction.
SVC T1: DF00..DFFF, request a supervisor exception.

Even when an operating system reports a process
fault from each under some configuration,
their architectural causes, debug behavior,
exception records and possible return paths differ.
Neither the current language nor a proposed
low-level translator may substitute one of
these instruction families for another
based only on the superficial outcome
"execution stopped".

The fixed BE prefix is part of the machine
instruction's bit-level identity, not a
mere spelling preference of the assembler.

PART SEVEN. A COMPLETE DECODER DIAGNOSTIC.

In a temporary Linux x86-64 analysis environment,
clang 17.0.0 assembled a Thumb text section
containing every .hword from BE00 through BEFF,
each exactly once in ascending order. LLVM
objdump 17.0.0 decoded the resulting ELF32
little-endian ARM object as Thumb instructions.

The decoder printed exactly 256
two-byte BKPT instructions.
It matched each original halfword and each
unsigned immediate from 0 through 255.
No unexplained mismatches were observed.
This is a foreign assembler/disassembler
diagnostic; it is not execution of
256 breakpoint events on ARM hardware.

The diagnostic source, object and disassembly
SHA256 digests are stored in the associated
source-based evidence receipt. Those transient
artifacts are not the finished English Major C
compiler or interpreter.

PART EIGHT. ALTERNATIVES AND THEIR COSTS.

Choice A: keep BKPT decoded-but-ungranted.
Advantage: permits exact disassembly inspection
without creating an underspecified debugging
interface. Cost: a faithful English translation
must stop when actual machine execution reaches
BKPT, until a versioned debug-event extension
is written.

Choice B: add a typed BKPT debug-event operation
in a future language grant. Advantage: can describe
real native debug events. Cost: requires actual
target/debug configuration, exceptions and
possibly debugger interface semantics.

Choice C: represent a deliberate failure
through UDF instead. Advantage: a different
and potentially simpler undefined-instruction
boundary on some targets. Cost: UDF is not
BKPT and cannot replace a native BKPT
without a documented semantic change.

Choice D: use SVC for a real OS service
or configured trap. Advantage: access to
the native OS exception/request boundary.
Cost: no general equivalence to a debug event;
requires a pinned operating-system ABI.

Choice E: use an ordinary explicit conditional
branch around the BKPT. Advantage: correctly
skips the instruction on the chosen path.
Cost: extra instruction, branch range,
flag dependence, and hardware-specific
prediction behavior.

Choice F: attempt to suppress BKPT merely
by putting it in a false IT instruction slot.
This is REJECTED as an architectural
misinterpretation: BKPT is unconditional.

Choice G: substitute an assembler mnemonic
or debugger convention for a complete
debug-state model. This is REJECTED:
printing "bkpt #imm" alone does not establish
what real system happens when it is executed.

No alternative has been shown universally
best for all debugging or application uses.

PART NINE. NEGATIVE CASES FOR FUTURE ACCEPTANCE.

A correct independent checker must:
  1. count exactly 256 unique halfwords;
  2. keep the literal immediate unsigned;
  3. reject a fabricated PC-relative branch
     target derived from imm8;
  4. decode BEFF as BKPT #255, not a
     negative immediate or a 32-bit instruction;
  5. reject treating BEAB as a kernel syscall
     solely because some debugger or semihosting
     convention uses that literal;
  6. reject the claim that false IT
     automatically suppresses BKPT;
  7. reject treating decoded BKPT as
     a permitted English Major C operation;
  8. distinguish the debug-event boundary
     from UDF and SVC exception causes;
  9. reject an invented unconditional
     software signal, handler or return
     without a concrete environment contract;
 10. refuse to claim that an LLVM disassembler
     performed a physical debug event;
 11. refuse to label this 256-encoding
     subgroup the entire Thumb ISA.

No English Major C executable-language
acceptance of these conditions has occurred.

END OF BE00..BEFF DECODE CONTRACT.
